Understanding someone else's PowerShell code when it uses unfamiliar modules can be overwhelming. The most effective strategy is to treat the script like a puzzle and use PowerShell's built-in discovery tools to inspect its components.
Here is how you can systematically deconstruct and understand unfamiliar PowerShell code:
## 1. Identify the Modules Being Used
Before figuring out what a command does, you need to find out where it came from.

* 
* Look for explicit imports: Check the top of the script for Import-Module or #requires -Modules statements, which explicitly list dependencies.
* Trace unknown cmdlets: If you see an unfamiliar command like Connect-MgGraph or Get-OktaUser, the noun prefix usually gives away the module (e.g., Mg for Microsoft Graph, Okta for Okta).
* Find the source command: If you have the module installed locally, run Get-Command <CmdletName> in your terminal to see its source module, version, and command type.
* 

## 2. Leverage PowerShell's Built-in Help System
PowerShell is self-documenting. If you can run the environment where the script executes, use these three commands to inspect the code's behavior:

* 
* Get-Help <CmdletName> -Detailed – Displays the official description, parameter explanations, and usage scenarios for the unfamiliar command.
* Get-Help <CmdletName> -Examples – Shows real-world use cases, which are often easier to understand than raw documentation.
* Get-Command -Module <ModuleName> – Lists every single command available inside that module so you can understand its broader capabilities.
* 

## 3. Trace Object Structures (The "Pipeline" Trick)
PowerShell passes rich objects, not just text, through its pipeline. If you don't know what data a variable or command is holding, pipe it into these discovery cmdlets:

* 
* Get-Member – Run $variable | Get-Member to see a complete list of properties and methods available on that object.
* Select-Object * – Run Get-UnfamiliarCmdlet | Select-Object -First 1 * to output a single object with all its properties visible, showing you exactly what data the script is manipulating.
* 

## 4. Use VS Code Tools
Reading raw text files is inefficient. Open the script in Visual Studio Code with the official PowerShell Extension installed:

* 
* Hover for Info: Hovering your mouse over any cmdlet or variable will show its definition, parameters, and types.
* Go to Definition: Pressing F12 on a function or variable will jump directly to where it was created in the script or module.
* Run in Integrated Terminal: Highlight a confusing snippet of code and press F8 to execute only that specific selection to see what it outputs.
* 

## 5. Check Online Registries
If a module isn't installed on your machine, search for it on the public [PowerShell Gallery](https://www.powershellgallery.com/). The gallery pages provide links to the project's source repository (usually GitHub), where you can read the documentation, view open issues, or look at the underlying source code.
